# Releasing a change a little at a time

This page is a checklist. It does not deploy this project, turn a change
on, or send people back to an older version. The automated checks, and a
person reviewing the change, still happen first. This checklist comes after
those. It does not replace them.

## Feature flag (a switch that hides a change)

Ship the change turned off. Turn it on for yourself first, then for a small
group, then for everyone. If something goes wrong, turn the switch off. The
previous behavior stays available until you remove it on purpose.

## Canary (a small share of real use)

Send the new version to a small part of real use. Watch one health check
you chose before you started. A health check is one signal that means "this
is working," such as errors staying low or one important page loading. If
the check stays good, send the new version to everyone. If it goes bad,
send everyone back to the previous version.

## Write these four things down first

- Who sees the change first.
- The one health check that means the change is well.
- Who can turn it off, and how, without writing a new code change.
- The previous version you can return to.

## What shipshape will not do yet

There is no deploy workflow in this setup. A workflow that deploys, checks
health, and returns to the previous version has to name one place the
project runs. This page does not pick that place. Ask for that workflow
only when you know where the project runs. Until then, follow the checklist
above with the tools you already use.
